    "title": "BharatNet to Bring Fiber Internet to Bihar Villages",
    "summary": "Bihar’s Villages Are Getting a Digital Lifeline: BharatNet Plans to Take Fiber Internet to Every Home What if the next big change in a Bihar village does not arrive on a new highway, but through a fiber cable running quietly into homes? That is the promise behind the revised BharatNet plan for Bihar, which aims [...]",
    "content": "Bihar’s Villages Are Getting a Digital Lifeline: BharatNet Plans to Take Fiber Internet to Every Home\n\nWhat if the next big change in a Bihar village does not arrive on a new highway, but through a fiber cable running quietly into homes?\n\nThat is the promise behind the revised BharatNet plan for Bihar, which aims to take high-speed internet beyond panchayat buildings and closer to ordinary rural households. The plan focuses on stronger village connectivity, direct fiber links for government institutions, and Fiber to the Home (FTTH) connections for families.\n\nAccording to the report published on September 10, 2026, the Bihar government’s Information Technology Department has started work on necessary changes to its existing agreement with the Centre. The network expansion is expected to begin by the end of the year.\n\nFor Bihar’s villages, this could mean more than faster browsing. It could change how students study, how families access government services, and how rural institutions work every day.\n\nWhat is changing with BharatNet in Bihar?\n\nUntil now, BharatNet connectivity in many villages has largely focused on reaching the gram panchayat level.\n\nThe revised approach aims to take that network further.\n\nUnder the plan, government institutions such as schools, police stations, anganwadi centres, post offices, health centres, cooperative institutions and Krishi Vigyan Kendras will be connected through fiber.\n\nMore importantly, the government also plans to expand Fiber to the Home, or FTTH.\n\nThat means rural households could eventually get direct fiber internet instead of relying only on mobile networks or distant public connectivity points.\n\nFor a state with a large rural population, that distinction matters.\n\nA connection at a panchayat building is useful. A connection inside a student’s home, a village clinic or a local business can be far more transformative.\n\nWhy reliable internet matters in rural Bihar\n\nFast internet is no longer simply an urban convenience.\n\nA student preparing for an examination may need online classes, study material or digital resources. A farmer may need access to online information and services. A family may need to complete a government-related process without travelling to a town.\n\nMeanwhile, health centres and other public institutions increasingly depend on digital systems.\n\nTherefore, improving rural connectivity can reduce the distance between Bihar’s villages and the services available elsewhere.\n\nThe biggest opportunity may come from reliability, not just speed.\n\nThe 99% network uptime target\n\nThe revised BharatNet model puts strong emphasis on keeping the network operational.\n\nThe plan targets around 99% network uptime.\n\nTo support this, the network will use modern technology and a ring topology.\n\nIn simple terms, the network can provide an alternative route when one part develops a technical problem. As a result, a failure in one section should not necessarily bring the entire connection down.\n\nThat reliability could be especially important for institutions that depend on continuous connectivity.\n\nA village health centre, school or government office cannot afford frequent interruptions when digital services become part of routine work.\n\nFrom schools to health centres: who could benefit?\n\nThe proposed fiber expansion covers several parts of village life.\n\nStudents and schools\n\nBetter internet could give rural students more consistent access to online learning resources.\n\nThis could also help schools use digital teaching tools more effectively.\n\nHowever, connectivity alone does not solve every educational challenge. Devices, digital skills and quality learning content still matter.\n\nRural healthcare\n\nHealth centres with dependable connectivity can participate more effectively in digital health services and online processes.\n\nFor people living far from larger towns, even reducing one unnecessary trip can make a practical difference.\n\nFarmers and rural businesses\n\nConnectivity can also support rural entrepreneurship.\n\nSmall businesses, service providers and local entrepreneurs increasingly need the internet for communication, payments, marketing and online transactions.\n\nMeanwhile, better connectivity can make it easier for rural businesses to reach customers beyond their immediate area.\n\nWhat could BharatNet mean for Bihar’s villages?\n\nThe larger story is about access.\n\nFor years, the digital divide was often described as a gap between cities and villages. But that gap is not only about whether a person has internet access.\n\nIt is also about the quality, reliability and usefulness of that connection.\n\nA young person in a Bihar village should not have to depend on an unstable connection simply because they live outside a major city.\n\nSimilarly, a rural entrepreneur should have a fair chance to participate in the digital economy.\n\nThat is why the shift from panchayat-level connectivity towards home-level fiber internet is significant.\n\nBihar’s digital future may begin at home\n\nThe real impact of BharatNet will not be measured only in kilometers of fiber laid.\n\nIt will eventually be visible in everyday moments.\n\nA student attending an online class from home. A family completing an online service without travelling. A village entrepreneur speaking to a customer in another city. A local institution staying connected when a nearby network route fails.\n\nThese are small changes individually. Together, they can reshape how rural Bihar interacts with the wider economy.\n\nAccording to the available details, the state has already started the process of making necessary changes to its existing arrangement with the Centre. The network expansion is expected to begin by the end of 2026.\n\nThe next important question is therefore not whether Bihar’s villages need better internet. They clearly do.\n\nIt is how quickly the fiber reaches homes, how consistently the network performs, and how effectively people turn that connectivity into education, opportunity and better access to services.\n\nBecause when the internet finally reaches the last home in a village, the cable itself is only the beginning. What people build with that connection will tell the bigger story.\n\nFollow Bihar Say at www.biharsay.com for more regular updates, untold stories, and meaningful developments from Bihar — and become part of our 15K+ community worldwide.",
