    "id": "bihars-makhana-business-is-booming-so-why-are-the-people-who-know-it-best-still-being-forced-to-migrate",
    "title": "Bihar Makhana Boom and the Hidden Cost of Migration",
    "summary": "Bihar’s makhana business is booming.So why are the people who know it best still being forced to migrate? Bihar’s makhana is reaching the world, but many families behind it still struggle to stay home. Makhana has become one of India’s most popular superfoods. However, its rising demand hides a painful reality in Bihar. Traditional processors [...]",
    "content": "Bihar’s makhana business is booming.So why are the people who know it best still being forced to migrate?\n\nBihar’s makhana is reaching the world, but many families behind it still struggle to stay home.\n\nMakhana has become one of India’s most popular superfoods. However, its rising demand hides a painful reality in Bihar.\n\nTraditional processors still face unstable incomes, limited working capital, and seasonal migration. As a result, many children miss school for months.\n\nIn Mithila, makhana represents more than farming. It reflects culture, skill, and family heritage.\n\n“Pag-pag pokhar, maachh-makhaan.”\n\nThis popular phrase captures Mithila’s deep connection with ponds, fish, and makhana. For generations, Mallah families have preserved the traditional processing craft.\n\nToday, however, that heritage faces serious economic pressure.\n\nThe Human Cost Behind Bihar’s Makhana Industry\n\nMakhana processing demands patience, strength, and experience. Artisans roast raw seeds in intense heat. Then, they crack them with wooden mallets.\n\nThis process requires precise timing and practiced hands. Therefore, machines cannot easily replace the knowledge passed through generations.\n\nYet many skilled processors cannot find enough work in their villages.\n\nDuring the makhana season, families often migrate for nearly six months. They travel to Purnea, Katihar, and parts of West Bengal.\n\nThey rent small rooms near work locations. Meanwhile, their children may remain away from school.\n\nA family earns money for the present. However, children can lose valuable months of education.\n\nThat trade-off reveals the hidden cost of Bihar’s makhana boom.\n\nWhy Farmers and Processors Struggle\n\nThe makhana supply chain depends on several connected groups. Farmers grow the crop. Processors transform the seeds. Traders and brands then take the product to consumers.\n\nHowever, each group faces different challenges.\n\nFarmers need reliable buyers and fair prices. Processors need raw seeds and timely working capital. Meanwhile, markets demand consistent quality and supply.\n\nWhen money arrives late, the entire chain suffers.\n\nFarmers may sell quickly at lower prices. Processors may leave their villages for seasonal work. Consequently, local communities lose both income and skilled labour.\n\nThe problem, therefore, involves more than market access. It also involves trust, timing, and financial support.\n\nHow Molu Jha Is Building a Local Solution\n\nFor Molu Jha, the makhana supply chain became a personal mission.\n\nHe saw farmers and processors struggling within the same system. Therefore, he began connecting them through a trust-based village network.\n\nHis approach focuses on three key needs:\n\nBetter access to raw makhana seeds\n\nFairer connections between farmers and buyers\n\nMore working opportunities for traditional processors\n\nThrough this network, farmers can reach local processors more directly. At the same time, processors can access the materials they need to continue working near home.\n\nThis model can reduce unnecessary migration. It can also help families protect their children’s education.\n\nMost importantly, it keeps more value within Bihar’s rural communities.\n\nProtecting a Skill That Bihar Cannot Afford to Lose\n\nTraditional makhana processing represents generations of knowledge. Artisans understand heat, timing, seed quality, and recovery rates through experience.\n\nHowever, low and uncertain incomes can push younger workers away from the craft.\n\nIf that continues, Bihar could lose more than a livelihood. It could lose an important part of Mithila’s cultural identity.\n\nMolu’s work also focuses on awareness. Farmers and processors need better information about grading, sizing, recovery ratios, and market prices.\n\nWith that knowledge, they can negotiate more confidently. They can also understand the real value of their labour.\n\nAs a result, transparency can strengthen the entire supply chain.\n\nKeeping Bihar’s Skilled Hands in Bihar\n\nEven a small reduction in seasonal migration can create a major social impact.\n\nFamilies can remain closer to their homes. Children can attend school more regularly. Communities can retain their skilled workers.\n\nFurthermore, local processing can create stronger village economies.\n\nMoney circulates within the community. Workers spend locally. Farmers gain more dependable market connections.\n\nThis creates a more resilient rural system.\n\nThe goal is not simply to sell more makhana. Instead, the goal is to build a supply chain that respects the people behind the product.\n\nThe Question Bihar Must Ask\n\nBihar’s makhana demand continues to grow. Consumers across India now recognise its nutritional value.\n\nHowever, market growth alone cannot guarantee social progress.\n\nThe real question is simple:\n\nCan Bihar build a makhana economy where farmers and processors receive a fairer share of the value they create?\n\nThe answer depends on stronger local networks, better financial access, and transparent market connections.\n\nIt also depends on recognising traditional processors as skilled professionals.\n\nThey do not merely perform manual labour. They carry knowledge that gives Bihar’s makhana its identity.\n\nA New Future for Bihar’s Makhana\n\nMolu Jha’s work points towards a more decentralised makhana supply chain.\n\nSuch a system can connect farmers, processors, and markets more fairly. It can also create more local employment.\n\nMost importantly, it can help families earn without leaving their villages for half the year.\n\nBihar’s makhana story is, therefore, not only about a growing superfood market.\n\nIt is about culture, migration, education, and dignity.\n\nIt is about protecting the hands that have preserved this craft for generations.\n\nIt is also about creating better opportunities for the next generation.\n\nFollow Bihar Say for more powerful stories from Bihar. Become part of our 13,000+ community worldwide and stay connected with the people, ideas, and changemakers shaping the state.",
