    "id": "nalanda-dongguk-university-revive-ancient-buddhist-knowledge-corridor",
    "title": "Nalanda & Dongguk University Revive Ancient Buddhist Knowledge Corridor",
    "summary": "From Rajgir to Seoul: Nalanda and Dongguk Reignite the Ancient Buddhist Knowledge Corridor When Ancient Wisdom Meets Modern Academia What happens when two civilisations connected by Buddhism meet again after centuries?History doesn’t whisper—it resonates. On a winter morning in Rajgir, Nalanda University became more than a campus. Instead, it turned into a bridge between India [...]",
    "content": "From Rajgir to Seoul: Nalanda and Dongguk Reignite the Ancient Buddhist Knowledge Corridor\n\nWhen Ancient Wisdom Meets Modern Academia\n\nWhat happens when two civilisations connected by Buddhism meet again after centuries?History doesn’t whisper—it resonates.\n\nOn a winter morning in Rajgir, Nalanda University became more than a campus. Instead, it turned into a bridge between India and Korea. More importantly, it revived an ancient Buddhist knowledge corridor that once shaped Asia’s intellectual soul.\n\nAs a result, Nalanda and Dongguk Universities didn’t just host a visit. They reignited a legacy.\n\nA Landmark Academic Moment in Bihar\n\nOn January 23, 2026, Nalanda University welcomed a 52-member delegation from Dongguk University, South Korea. Notably, the visit marked one of the most meaningful academic engagements in recent years.\n\nThe delegation was led by Venerable Donkwan, Chairperson of the Dongguk University Foundation, and Prof Jaewoong Yun, President of Dongguk University. Together, they brought spiritual authority and academic leadership to Bihar.\n\nMeanwhile, the atmosphere reflected shared purpose rather than formality. Therefore, the engagement felt deeply human and future-focused.\n\nArt, Spirituality, and Living Buddhist Traditions\n\nThe visit began with the inauguration of an exhibition of Thangka paintings and Buddhist artefacts. Venerable Donkwan personally opened the exhibition.\n\nMoreover, the presence of Bhikshu Dovung, a renowned Korean monk-artist, added rare depth. His artwork didn’t just hang on walls. Instead, it told stories of meditation, discipline, and devotion.\n\nConsequently, the exhibition became a living dialogue between art and spirituality.\n\nNalanda’s Vision: Global Dialogue Rooted in Dharma\n\nWhile welcoming the delegation, Prof Sachin Chaturvedi, Vice-Chancellor of Nalanda University, shared a powerful vision.\n\nHe highlighted Nalanda’s goal to reclaim its historic role as a global centre of learning. At the same time, he connected ancient Buddhist wisdom with modern global challenges.\n\nSpecifically, discussions focused on:\n\nBuddhist pilgrimage studies\n\nPhilosophy and ethics\n\nCultural conservation\n\nSustainable Development Goals\n\nGlobal peace and coexistence\n\nTherefore, Nalanda positioned itself as both ancient and forward-looking.\n\nInside the Closed-Door Academic Roundtable\n\nBehind the scenes, senior leaders, deans, and faculty members from both universities engaged in focused dialogue.\n\nThey explored joint research initiatives, faculty exchanges, and student mobility programmes. Furthermore, they discussed long-term frameworks for sustained academic cooperation.\n\nAs a result, both sides moved beyond symbolism. They committed to structure and strategy.\n\nStudents at the Heart of Cultural Exchange\n\nAt the Sushma Swaraj Auditorium, the spotlight shifted to students.\n\nInternational students performed Buddhavacana chanting, creating a deeply immersive experience. Meanwhile, presentations highlighted Buddhist pilgrimage sites in Rajgir.\n\nAdditionally, Korean delegates interacted directly with students. These conversations built curiosity, empathy, and shared learning.\n\nThus, the partnership felt alive at every level.\n\nWhat This Partnership Unlocks Next\n\nBuilding on an existing Memorandum of Understanding, both institutions agreed on concrete next steps.\n\nThese include:\n\nStructured faculty and student exchange programmes\n\nAnnual faculty visits\n\nJoint research projects\n\nAn international conference hosted alternately in India and South Korea\n\nTherefore, this partnership now has momentum, clarity, and purpose.\n\nWhy This Matters for Bihar and the World\n\nThis collaboration places Bihar at the centre of global Buddhist scholarship once again.\n\nMore importantly, it reframes Bihar’s image—from history alone to active global leadership. In addition, it strengthens cultural diplomacy between India and South Korea.\n\nUltimately, Nalanda’s revival is not symbolic. It is strategic, spiritual, and global.\n\nStay Connected to Stories That Matter\n\nWant more such powerful stories shaping Bihar’s global identity?Then follow 👉 www.biharsay.com for regular updates and become part of our 13,000+ strong worldwide community 🌍\n\nBihar Say is Bihar’s leading apolitical media and storytelling platform. We are reshaping the state’s image through stories on culture, business, and grassroots change. With top LinkedIn impressions, partnerships with platforms like DailyHunt, and our flagship podcast BiharCast, we amplify voices from the heart of Bihar—where tradition meets transformation.",
